 But the individual chose B. However, the reason does not specify whether it is the sum of absolute differences or signed differences. Given the ambiguity, the key point is that the sum of outcome differences (in absolute or signed) is a fixed value across both lotteries in this case. Therefore, the decision reason would not favor one over the other based on outcome differences — it would be indifferent. Thus, the reason cannot be applied to distinguish between A and B based on outcome differences, as the sum is the same. However, if interpreted as the sum of differences in losses (i.e., the difference in the negative outcomes), then |(-3000) - (-4000)| = 1000, which is the same for both lotteries. Therefore, the sum of pairwise outcome differences is equal for both lotteries. Hence, the decision reason would not favor either. So, the decision reason is not sufficient to make a choice between A and B based on outcome differences — it would be indifferent. Thus, the reason cannot be applied to make a decision in this case.",
